select m.id, 1, $L$What Is an API?$L$, $L$12 minute read$L$, $L$An API is how two pieces of software talk to each other. Before you can test one, you need a clear mental model of what an API actually is and why it exists.$L$, array[$L$Explain what an API is in plain language$L$, $L$Describe the client–server relationship$L$, $L$Identify where APIs sit in a real application$L$], $L$## The plain-English definition
An **API** (Application Programming Interface) is a contract that lets one program request something from another and get a predictable response back. You don't need to know *how* the other program works — only what to send and what you'll get.

## An everyday analogy
Ordering at a restaurant: you (the **client**) give your order to the waiter (the **API**), who takes it to the kitchen (the **server**) and brings back your food (the **response**). You never enter the kitchen — the waiter is the defined interface.

## Where APIs live
- The **frontend** calls APIs to fetch and save data.
- The **backend** exposes those APIs and talks to databases.
- Apps also call **third-party** APIs (payments, maps, email).

## Why testers care
Most business logic and integration risk lives at the API layer — below the UI. Testing there is faster, more stable, and catches bugs the UI can hide.$L$, $L$Open a weather app. When you type a city, it sends `GET /weather?city=Sheffield` to a weather service, which replies with JSON (temperature, conditions, forecast). The app draws the screen from that data — the UI is just a view of an API response.$L$, $L$- Thinking an API is "a website" — it returns data, not pages
- Believing you must be a developer to test APIs
- Assuming the UI and the API always show the same thing (they often don't)$L$, $L$APIs are where the real contract between systems lives. If you can read and test an API, you can find bugs long before they ever reach a screen.$L$, $L$In one or two sentences, describe an app you use daily and name one thing it almost certainly requests from an API.$L$, $L$In the client–server model, which side sends the request?$L$, $L$In the client–server model, which side sends the request? (Answer: the client)$L$, array[$L$I can explain what an API is without jargon$L$, $L$I can describe the client–server relationship$L$, $L$I can point to where APIs sit in an app$L$]
