select m.id, 3, $L$REST Principles & Resources$L$, $L$13 minute read$L$, $L$REST is the style most modern APIs follow. Knowing its principles tells you what to expect from an endpoint before you even test it.$L$, array[$L$Explain what REST means$L$, $L$Describe resources and how URLs represent them$L$, $L$Recognise statelessness and its testing impact$L$], $L$## REST in one line
**RE**presentational **S**tate **T**ransfer: you act on **resources** using standard HTTP methods.

## Resources and URLs
- Collection: `/users`
- Single item: `/users/42`
- Sub-resource: `/users/42/orders`
Nouns, not verbs — the *method* is the verb.

## Key principles
- **Statelessness** — each request is independent
- **Uniform interface** — consistent methods and status codes
- **Client–server separation**

## Why it matters
Once you know an API is RESTful you can predict behaviour: `GET /users/42` reads, `DELETE /users/42` removes, a missing user returns `404`. Predictability = testability.$L$, $L$A bookshop API: `GET /books` (list), `GET /books/12` (one), `POST /books` (add), `PUT /books/12` (replace), `DELETE /books/12` (remove). If `GET /getBook?id=12` appears instead, the API isn't cleanly RESTful — worth noting in your approach.$L$, $L$- Expecting verbs in URLs (`/createUser`) — REST uses nouns + methods
- Assuming a session is remembered between requests
- Treating every API as REST when some are RPC or GraphQL$L$, $L$RESTful design is a gift to testers: it makes behaviour predictable. When an API breaks its own conventions, that unpredictability is a risk worth flagging.$L$, $L$Design the five REST endpoints (method + path) for managing "projects".$L$, $L$Should REST URLs contain nouns or verbs, and why?$L$, $L$In REST, should URLs contain nouns or verbs? (Answer: nouns — the HTTP method is the verb)$L$, array[$L$I can explain REST simply$L$, $L$I can design resource URLs$L$, $L$I understand statelessness$L$]
from public.modules m join public.courses c on c.id = m.course_id
where c.slug = 'api-testing-masterclass' and m.module_number = 1
on conflict (module_id, lesson_number) do nothing;

insert into public.lessons (module_id, lesson_number, title, estimated_time, lesson_overview, learning_objectives, lesson_notes, worked_example, common_mistakes, real_world_tip, exercise, reflection_question, knowledge_check, completion_checklist)
select m.id, 4, $L$Where API Testing Fits in the SDLC$L$, $L$12 minute read$L$, $L$API testing is one of the highest-value activities a tester can do. This lesson shows why, and where it fits in the test pyramid and delivery lifecycle.$L$, array[$L$Place API testing on the test pyramid$L$, $L$Explain the value of shifting testing left$L$, $L$Describe when API tests run in delivery$L$], $L$## The test pyramid
- **Unit** (many, fast) — individual functions
- **API / integration** (fewer, still fast) — services talking
- **UI / end-to-end** (few, slow, brittle) — the whole app

API tests sit in the middle: far more stable and faster than UI, far broader than unit.

## Shift left
APIs are often built **before** the UI. Testing them early finds integration and data bugs when they're cheapest to fix.

## Where they run
- During development
- In CI/CD on every commit (via Newman — Module 8)
- In regression suites and monitoring$L$, $L$A team's checkout kept failing flaky UI tests. Moving the core checks to the API layer (`POST /cart`, `POST /orders`) made them fast and stable — and caught a real tax-calculation bug days before release.$L$, $L$- Only testing through the UI and ignoring the API layer
- Waiting for the UI to exist before testing anything
- Duplicating every UI test at the API level instead of choosing the right layer$L$, $L$If a test can be done at the API layer instead of the UI, it usually should — faster, more stable, and it runs on every build.$L$, $L$List two checks that are better done at the API layer than through the UI, and say why.$L$, $L$Why are API tests generally more stable than end-to-end UI tests?$L$, $L$On the test pyramid, are API tests faster or slower than UI/end-to-end tests? (Answer: faster and more stable)$L$, array[$L$I can place API testing on the pyramid$L$, $L$I can explain shifting left$L$, $L$I know when API tests run$L$]
from public.modules m join public.courses c on c.id = m.course_id
where c.slug = 'api-testing-masterclass' and m.module_number = 1
on conflict (module_id, lesson_number) do nothing;

insert into public.lessons (module_id, lesson_number, title, estimated_time, lesson_overview, learning_objectives, lesson_notes, worked_example, common_mistakes, real_world_tip, exercise, reflection_question, knowledge_check, completion_checklist)
select m.id, 5, $L$API Testing vs UI Testing$L$, $L$11 minute read$L$, $L$API and UI testing answer different questions. Knowing which to use — and when — is what separates a strategic tester from a button-clicker.$L$, array[$L$Contrast API and UI testing$L$, $L$Choose the right layer for a given check$L$, $L$Explain why the UI can mask API problems$L$], $L$## Different questions
- **API testing** asks: *is the logic and data correct?*
- **UI testing** asks: *can a user actually do this on screen?*

## Trade-offs
| | API | UI |
|---|---|---|
| Speed | Fast | Slow |
| Stability | High | Lower |
| Setup | Low | Higher |
| User realism | Lower | High |

## The UI can hide API bugs
A screen might show "Success" while the API returned the wrong data. Only testing the API reveals it.

## Rule of thumb
Push logic, data and edge-case checks **down** to the API. Keep the UI for a few true end-to-end journeys.$L$, $L$A discount API returned `£0.00` off for a valid code, but the UI defaulted to full price with no error — so UI testing "passed". An API test asserting the discounted total caught it immediately.$L$, $L$- Doing everything through the UI because it "feels real"
- Assuming a green UI means the data underneath is correct
- Never testing the API independently of the screen$L$, $L$When the UI and the API disagree, trust the API response — then find out why the UI hid the truth.$L$, $L$Give one example of a bug the UI could show as "passing" while the API is actually wrong.$L$, $L$Which layer better answers "is the returned data correct?" and why?$L$, $L$Which layer better answers "is the returned data correct?" (Answer: the API layer)$L$, array[$L$I can contrast API and UI testing$L$, $L$I can choose the right layer$L$, $L$I understand how UIs can mask API bugs$L$]
from public.modules m join public.courses c on c.id = m.course_id
where c.slug = 'api-testing-masterclass' and m.module_number = 1
on conflict (module_id, lesson_number) do nothing;

