select m.id, 3, $L$PUT, PATCH & Updating$L$, $L$14 minute read$L$, $L$Updating a resource can mean replacing it wholesale (`PUT`) or changing just part of it (`PATCH`). Confusing the two is one of the most common sources of data-loss bugs.$L$, array[$L$Contrast `PUT` (full replace) with `PATCH` (partial update)$L$, $L$Predict what happens to omitted fields for each$L$, $L$Verify an update by re-reading the resource$L$], $L$## PUT replaces the whole resource
`PUT /users/1` sends the **complete** representation. Fields you omit are typically **wiped** — `PUT` means "make the resource look exactly like this". `PUT` is **idempotent**: repeating the same `PUT` leaves the same result.

## PATCH updates part of a resource
`PATCH /users/1` sends **only the fields you want to change**. Everything else is left alone. It is the safer choice when you only mean to touch one field.

## The key testing risk
Sending a partial body to a `PUT` and expecting the other fields to survive. They often won't — this is a classic data-loss bug.

## What testers check
- Status `200 OK` (or `204 No Content` if no body is returned).
- After a `PUT`, omitted fields are cleared/defaulted as designed.
- After a `PATCH`, untouched fields are **unchanged**.
- Both are **idempotent**: repeating the request gives the same final state.$L$, $L$Start with `{"id":1,"name":"Ada","email":"ada@x.com"}`. Send `PATCH /users/1` with `{"name":"Ada Lovelace"}` → `200 OK`, and a follow-up `GET` shows the email is **still there**. Send `PUT /users/1` with only `{"name":"Ada Lovelace"}` and the email may now be **gone** — because `PUT` replaced the whole record.$L$, $L$- Using `PUT` with a partial body and losing the omitted fields
- Assuming `PATCH` and `PUT` behave the same way
- Checking only the update response and never re-reading the resource$L$, $L$Always re-`GET` after an update and diff the whole object against what you expected. The bug is rarely in the field you changed — it is in the field you didn't mean to touch.$L$, $L$In Postman, `GET` a resource, then `PATCH` one field. Re-`GET` and confirm your field changed while every other field stayed identical.$L$, $L$If a client only wants to change a user's email, which method is safer and why?$L$, $L$Which method replaces an entire resource, wiping fields you omit? (Answer: PUT — PATCH only changes the fields you send)$L$, array[$L$I can explain the difference between `PUT` and `PATCH`$L$, $L$I can predict what happens to omitted fields$L$, $L$I verify updates by re-reading the resource$L$]
from public.modules m join public.courses c on c.id = m.course_id
where c.slug = 'api-testing-masterclass' and m.module_number = 2
